.TH STYXBENCH 1
.SH NAME
styxbench \- time a Styx round trip against a raw TCP exchange
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B styxbench
[
.BI -n " count"
]
[
.BI -s " size"
]
.I rawaddr file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Styxbench
times
.I count
exchanges of
.I size
bytes three ways against the same server, and prints microseconds each
and the ratios: a write-and-read on a bare TCP connection to
.IR rawaddr ;
sequential reads of
.IR file ,
which is on a mounted namespace; and reads of
.I file
at offsets a megabyte apart, so that the mount driver's readahead
cannot answer one from another.
.PP
It exists for the section of
.B doc/inferno-cloud-modern.ms
that proposes to measure the claim the rest of the plan rests on: that
distribution in the operating system is cheaper than distribution
assembled above one.  A namespace mounted across a network is the form
that claim takes, and a Styx round trip is its price.
.SH BUGS
.B "The comparison it makes is not yet a fair one, and the numbers it
.B "prints should not be quoted."
The Styx half is answered by the kernel \(em
.IR exportfs (3)
reading a file such as
.B /dev/zero
in C \(em while the raw half is answered by
.IR tcpserve (1),
a Limbo program paying Dis interpretation on every exchange.  So the
ratio is dominated by which language the server is written in, not by
the protocol.  Measured that way Styx comes out at 0.4 times the cost
of raw TCP across three runs of 3000 iterations, stable to one decimal
\(em and an abstraction cannot cost less than the transport it is
carried on, which is how the flaw was noticed rather than published.
.PP
A sound version needs both halves answered by the same kind of server:
either a raw echo served by the kernel, which does not exist, or the
Styx half served by a Limbo file server so that both pay Dis.  Until
then this measures the tool and not the question.
.PP
The transport is QEMU's user-mode networking, which is jittery: at 300
iterations the ratio ranged from 0.3 to 1.3 across runs, and only at
3000 did it settle.  The plan's own version of this experiment is
between two instances on a real network, which is also where it should
be repeated.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/styxbench.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR tcpserve (1),
.IR styxlisten (1),
.IR export (4)
